# Loom resync pin list (read by .loom/scripts/resync-installed.sh -> is_ignored,
# and by Loom's scripts/install/migrate-consumer.sh). One installed-surface
# path per line, relative to the repo root; `#` starts a comment.
#
# package.json: deliberately deleted, not missing (issue #90).
#
# Loom's installer ships defaults/package.json as workspace scaffolding whose
# `test`, `check:ci`, `check:all` and `lint` scripts echo a message and
# `exit 0`. This repo tracks no JavaScript or TypeScript, and its charter is
# "Verification is the product: no claim without a testbench" (CLAUDE.md). A
# root `npm test` that returns 0 having compiled, simulated and checked
# nothing is a false verification entry point -- and Loom's Auditor role
# probes for package.json by name as a build+test entry point
# (.claude/commands/loom/auditor.md), so an agent following that documented
# discovery path would find it before the real entry points
# (verification/run.sh, layout/run.sh, layout/sdf-verify.sh; README.md).
#
# `.loom/install-metadata.json` still lists package.json as an installed
# surface, so this pin keeps a future resync or re-install from writing the
# stub back. Note that `resync-installed.sh` prints an advisory
# "pin had no effect: 'package.json'" while the file is absent -- its
# `resync_workspace_stub_version()` returns early before consulting this list
# when there is no package.json to inspect. That line is cosmetic (it does not
# affect the resync exit status); the pin is what makes the deletion stick if
# the stub ever is restored.
package.json
